WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:30.840
- Hi everyone, Sal Khan
here from Khan Academy.

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:33.650
Welcome to our daily homeroom live stream.

00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:34.793
For those of y'all who are new,

00:00:34.793 --> 00:00:36.577
this is something that we started doing,

00:00:36.577 --> 00:00:38.594
it feels like a lifetime ago now,

00:00:38.594 --> 00:00:40.286
almost two and a half months ago

00:00:40.286 --> 00:00:42.430
when we started seeing school closures,

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:43.273
and we realized that, you know,

00:00:43.273 --> 00:00:44.776
it's our duty as a not-for-profit

00:00:44.776 --> 00:00:46.780
to step up even more and figure out

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:48.036
how we can not only keep people learning,

00:00:48.036 --> 00:00:49.694
but how can we stay connected

00:00:49.694 --> 00:00:51.293
in this time of social distancing?

00:00:51.293 --> 00:00:53.881
So this is just a place to
have interesting conversations

00:00:53.881 --> 00:00:55.131
about education,

00:00:55.131 --> 00:00:57.590
about the crises in the world,

00:00:57.590 --> 00:00:58.830
and anything else.

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:00.603
And we have a very, very
exciting guest today.

00:01:00.603 --> 00:01:02.360
But before we get to our guest,

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:03.740
I'll make my standard announcement,

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:05.390
reminding everyone that Khan Academy

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:06.736
is a not-for-profit organization.

00:01:06.736 --> 00:01:09.467
We can only exist due to
philanthropic donations.

00:01:09.467 --> 00:01:11.658
We were running at a deficit even

00:01:11.658 --> 00:01:13.280
before the covid crisis.

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:14.600
And you could imagine with our usage

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:16.430
being two to three X of normal,

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:17.370
and we're trying to accelerate

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:18.651
a whole series of programs to,

00:01:18.651 --> 00:01:20.403
keep all of y'all learning,

00:01:20.403 --> 00:01:22.239
to support teachers and parents.

00:01:22.239 --> 00:01:24.830
And, to support everyone
as we go to this very,

00:01:24.830 --> 00:01:28.171
unusual back to school
that we're likely to face.

00:01:28.171 --> 00:01:30.379
The only reason why
we're able to do that is,

00:01:30.379 --> 00:01:32.160
with support from folks like yourself,

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:33.470
I do want to make a special shout out

00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:34.684
to several corporations
that have stepped up.

00:01:34.684 --> 00:01:37.896
Bank of America, Google.org, AT&amp;T, fastly,

00:01:37.896 --> 00:01:39.040
and Novartis.

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:40.490
All stepped up in the last few weeks.

00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:42.130
And this is above and beyond the many

00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:43.804
other philanthropists, corporate partners,

00:01:43.804 --> 00:01:46.180
foundations that have
supported Khan Academy

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:47.585
through the years that
have put us in a position

00:01:47.585 --> 00:01:49.479
to be able to support folks

00:01:49.479 --> 00:01:51.040
through a crisis like this.

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:52.810
So thank you to all of them,

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:54.616
but we are still running at a deficit

00:01:54.616 --> 00:01:56.491
and there's still a lot that we could do.

00:01:56.491 --> 00:01:58.062
And so if you're in a position to do so,

00:01:58.062 --> 00:02:00.123
please think about donating.

00:02:00.123 --> 00:02:01.634
So with that said,

00:02:01.634 --> 00:02:03.865
I'm excited to introduce our guest,

00:02:03.865 --> 00:02:06.540
Stu Schmill, who's the Dean of admissions

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:10.310
and student financial services at MIT.

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:11.453
Stu, welcome.

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:13.293
- Thank you, Sal.

00:02:13.293 --> 00:02:14.126
Nice to see you.

00:02:14.126 --> 00:02:18.026
- Yeah, you know, when we
had you on the guest list,

00:02:18.026 --> 00:02:18.886
I was, "What are we gonna talk about?

00:02:18.886 --> 00:02:19.969
There's nothing interesting

00:02:19.969 --> 00:02:20.802
(laughs)

00:02:20.802 --> 00:02:23.380
going on in college admissions

00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:24.780
or testing or colleges.

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:28.000
It's just all same, same,
same as always, right?

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:29.970
- Yeah, It's been very, boring

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:33.179
and even, nine to five come home

00:02:33.179 --> 00:02:35.948
and actually it's not even
come home anymore (laughs)

00:02:35.948 --> 00:02:38.822
- Right, stay home, you
don't have to go, yeah.

00:02:38.822 --> 00:02:43.822
It sounds like your attic
room and all seriousness,

00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:46.926
obviously, a lot has obviously,

00:02:46.926 --> 00:02:50.500
if the sarcasm wasn't
super obvious to anyone,

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:52.684
a lot has obviously been
going on in the world.

00:02:52.684 --> 00:02:55.357
I don't know, maybe tell
me from your vantage point,

00:02:55.357 --> 00:02:57.890
you know, how has it evolved?

00:02:57.890 --> 00:02:59.150
How has your thinking evolved?

00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:00.520
What are you seeing from students?

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:01.500
What are their concerns

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:03.090
and, just how do you see

00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:06.488
this coming back to school changing

00:03:06.488 --> 00:03:09.670
or evolving for a place like MIT?

00:03:09.670 --> 00:03:14.550
- Yeah, so I think, the pandemic

00:03:16.210 --> 00:03:17.123
while it had been building

00:03:17.123 --> 00:03:20.059
for some time over the
beginning part of this year,

00:03:20.059 --> 00:03:23.950
I think really, took hold in our minds

00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:25.950
in terms of changes that we were going

00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:28.620
to need to make over the course

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:31.448
of this spring relatively quickly.

00:03:31.448 --> 00:03:33.137
Really get to the end of February,

00:03:33.137 --> 00:03:34.903
beginning of March,

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:38.181
and we made some very,
very quick decisions

00:03:38.181 --> 00:03:43.140
on what our education was
gonna look like this spring.

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:45.907
And, universities are not always

00:03:45.907 --> 00:03:47.840
or haven't always been known

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:50.023
for very quick decision making.

00:03:51.170 --> 00:03:54.540
And I was actually very impressed with,

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:57.592
the speed at which we
were able to carry out,

00:03:57.592 --> 00:03:59.611
some of the changes that we did.

00:03:59.611 --> 00:04:02.816
But also I'm very impressed with,

00:04:02.816 --> 00:04:06.783
I know certainly the
people on my campus at MIT,

00:04:06.783 --> 00:04:09.880
and I think this is true universally,

00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:14.465
is how much we kept the
student at the center

00:04:14.465 --> 00:04:17.969
of all of the decisions
that were being made

00:04:17.969 --> 00:04:19.993
and tried to make decisions that were

00:04:19.993 --> 00:04:23.660
in the best interest of the students.

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:26.210
And, so certainly the decision

00:04:26.210 --> 00:04:27.720
to send our students home,

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:29.800
or at least those that
were able to go home,

00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:31.390
those who would be safer

00:04:31.390 --> 00:04:32.790
from a health point of view,

00:04:34.315 --> 00:04:36.249
sending them home very quickly was really

00:04:36.249 --> 00:04:38.073
in their best interest.

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:42.563
Standing up, continuing
our education along

00:04:42.563 --> 00:04:44.653
in online version.

00:04:45.490 --> 00:04:48.427
Very quickly, also trying to ensure

00:04:48.427 --> 00:04:50.357
that students had the ability to continue

00:04:50.357 --> 00:04:53.533
to make progress towards their degree.

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:55.670
But also there were students

00:04:55.670 --> 00:04:58.240
that had very particular circumstances.

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:00.493
So right now there are still
a few hundred students,

00:05:00.493 --> 00:05:03.445
undergraduate students who
are living on the MIT campus

00:05:03.445 --> 00:05:06.726
because it's a better
environment for them,

00:05:06.726 --> 00:05:08.860
than anywhere else that

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:10.310
they might have been able to go to.

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:15.310
And, really, a lot of
decisions that have been made,

00:05:17.961 --> 00:05:20.070
were really made with the students

00:05:20.070 --> 00:05:20.995
at the center of this.

00:05:20.995 --> 00:05:22.433
And I know going forward

00:05:22.433 --> 00:05:24.010
because the last few months

00:05:24.010 --> 00:05:28.100
we've been planning on
what's going to happen,

00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:29.460
as we get near the fall.

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:30.950
And maybe you

00:05:30.950 --> 00:05:31.783
or others,

00:05:31.783 --> 00:05:32.719
might have questions about that,

00:05:32.719 --> 00:05:35.200
as we go along here.

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:38.429
We're also thinking about
what is gonna be best

00:05:38.429 --> 00:05:42.160
for our students and our entire community.

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:44.190
Both from a health point of view

00:05:44.190 --> 00:05:45.523
and then educationally.

00:05:46.359 --> 00:05:47.853
- And what do you see the scenarios?

00:05:47.853 --> 00:05:49.510
I know, I mean I'm eager to talk

00:05:49.510 --> 00:05:52.470
about how even admissions
has had to evolve

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:53.840
or might have to evolve going forward,

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:55.708
but just your vantage point
in the university itself,

00:05:55.708 --> 00:05:58.100
what are the continuity
plans you're hearing

00:05:58.100 --> 00:05:59.950
about for this coming back to school?

00:06:00.990 --> 00:06:05.813
- Well, we certainly know
that, with whatever happens

00:06:05.813 --> 00:06:08.560
and whatever decisions
that are made by colleges

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:10.660
and universities around
what their educations

00:06:10.660 --> 00:06:11.950
are going to look like,

00:06:11.950 --> 00:06:13.310
it's going to look very different

00:06:13.310 --> 00:06:14.424
than it did last fall.

00:06:14.424 --> 00:06:18.130
The virus is still going to be with us

00:06:18.130 --> 00:06:21.010
and, it's still very contagious

00:06:21.010 --> 00:06:22.710
and, you have to be very careful

00:06:22.710 --> 00:06:26.500
about, very dense environments.

00:06:26.500 --> 00:06:29.917
And universities are
very dense environments.

00:06:29.917 --> 00:06:32.701
And, I think, we,

00:06:32.701 --> 00:06:36.190
along with many other really all colleges

00:06:36.190 --> 00:06:38.640
and universities, acknowledge

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:42.140
and recognize that, online,

00:06:42.140 --> 00:06:46.053
or rather in-person education
is extremely valuable.

00:06:46.889 --> 00:06:50.570
And we want to see
students come to campus.

00:06:50.570 --> 00:06:51.900
We just have to be able to do that

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:52.949
in a responsible way.

00:06:52.949 --> 00:06:55.678
And what that will ultimately look like,

00:06:55.678 --> 00:06:57.603
it's not yet clear.

00:06:57.603 --> 00:07:00.140
It's not clear that
we'll be able to have all

00:07:00.140 --> 00:07:02.000
of our students back on campus

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:05.100
or some of our students on campus

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:09.263
and where that line is drawn.

00:07:09.263 --> 00:07:14.263
So I think that, regardless
of ultimately what happens,

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:16.000
it will be different

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:18.580
because even if students are on campus,

00:07:18.580 --> 00:07:20.605
there will be different expectations

00:07:20.605 --> 00:07:23.140
for what we'll be able to do

00:07:24.269 --> 00:07:26.874
in terms of, living in the dorms,

00:07:26.874 --> 00:07:28.886
will have to be a little
bit less dense perhaps

00:07:28.886 --> 00:07:30.218
than we have been in the past.

00:07:30.218 --> 00:07:34.230
In the classroom, we'll
have to be less dense.

00:07:34.230 --> 00:07:37.942
They'll be, guidance around,

00:07:37.942 --> 00:07:42.280
or guidelines around social distancing

00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:44.319
and wearing face coverings.

00:07:44.319 --> 00:07:47.490
Will there be parties and sporting events

00:07:47.490 --> 00:07:48.524
and all the rest of that?

00:07:48.524 --> 00:07:51.940
We're not gonna be able
to have the same kind

00:07:51.940 --> 00:07:54.813
of large event programming,

00:07:54.813 --> 00:07:59.050
that colleges are,

00:07:59.050 --> 00:08:00.618
well in some cases famous for, certainly.

00:08:00.618 --> 00:08:04.420
But it's one of the things that students

00:08:04.420 --> 00:08:05.450
really love about college.

00:08:05.450 --> 00:08:07.610
So those things are gonna
change a little bit in the fall.

00:08:07.610 --> 00:08:10.157
- And what are you hearing from the class

00:08:10.157 --> 00:08:11.360
that you just admitted?

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:12.831
The people who would be freshman?

00:08:12.831 --> 00:08:14.929
I'm hearing in the general press,

00:08:14.929 --> 00:08:16.717
a lot of students thinking about,

00:08:16.717 --> 00:08:19.840
"Well, you know, freshman
year maybe I take a year off

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:21.050
or take a gap year".

00:08:21.050 --> 00:08:22.190
How accommodating are you,

00:08:22.190 --> 00:08:23.023
all of that type of thing?

00:08:23.023 --> 00:08:24.360
And how are y'all advising students

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:26.136
who are thinking that through?

00:08:26.136 --> 00:08:27.964
- Yeah, so students,

00:08:27.964 --> 00:08:30.330
right now we're getting
a lot of questions.

00:08:30.330 --> 00:08:31.804
Students wanna know what's gonna happen.

00:08:31.804 --> 00:08:35.913
I think most everybody is,

00:08:36.920 --> 00:08:39.280
really hoping that they're gonna be able

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:41.909
to come to campus, in the fall.

00:08:41.909 --> 00:08:43.302
We're hoping the same thing.

00:08:43.302 --> 00:08:45.975
So we're getting a lot
of questions about that.

00:08:45.975 --> 00:08:48.690
And we have had students ask us,

00:08:48.690 --> 00:08:49.957
or say that,

00:08:49.957 --> 00:08:53.930
"Well, look, if I can't
come to campus in the fall,

00:08:53.930 --> 00:08:56.087
then I may take a gap year

00:08:56.087 --> 00:08:58.895
or I may defer my enrollment".

00:08:58.895 --> 00:09:00.589
Thing about that is,

00:09:00.589 --> 00:09:02.761
I think students are saying that

00:09:02.761 --> 00:09:05.350
without really even knowing,

00:09:05.350 --> 00:09:07.540
well, what are their
other options going to be?

00:09:07.540 --> 00:09:12.152
So take a gap year to do what?

00:09:12.152 --> 00:09:13.224
So that's not clear.

00:09:13.224 --> 00:09:16.782
And also, I think students,

00:09:16.782 --> 00:09:21.345
particularly new students are thinking,

00:09:21.345 --> 00:09:24.705
well either on campus and
if it's not on campus,

00:09:24.705 --> 00:09:29.705
they're thinking that
the online experience

00:09:29.780 --> 00:09:31.497
that we may be able to offer in the fall

00:09:31.497 --> 00:09:36.012
is going to be like what
their online experience

00:09:36.012 --> 00:09:38.054
has been in high school.

00:09:38.054 --> 00:09:40.316
And I don't think that's true.

00:09:40.316 --> 00:09:43.722
In fact, I know that our faculty

00:09:43.722 --> 00:09:46.820
and many of our other
instructional professionals

00:09:46.820 --> 00:09:49.073
are working very hard to ensure that,

00:09:50.390 --> 00:09:53.505
any of the online tools that we have,

00:09:53.505 --> 00:09:57.660
for teaching students are gonna be,

00:09:57.660 --> 00:09:58.645
really very high quality

00:09:58.645 --> 00:10:02.030
and very different than
what students have seen

00:10:02.030 --> 00:10:04.763
and experienced this spring.

00:10:06.915 --> 00:10:08.713
And, I think in any scenario,

00:10:08.713 --> 00:10:12.296
even when scenario where
students are on campus,

00:10:12.296 --> 00:10:15.150
there's going to be some online learning,

00:10:15.150 --> 00:10:16.210
that will be happening,

00:10:16.210 --> 00:10:17.664
particularly again because,

00:10:17.664 --> 00:10:21.720
we have to limit the size
of classes, et cetera.

00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:23.529
So I think the quality of
that online instruction

00:10:23.529 --> 00:10:26.720
is going to improve.

00:10:26.720 --> 00:10:28.644
And so students are,

00:10:28.644 --> 00:10:31.776
I don't think they have enough
information yet to know,

00:10:31.776 --> 00:10:34.240
whether they really do want

00:10:34.240 --> 00:10:37.187
to defer their enrollment or not.

00:10:37.187 --> 00:10:38.287
For our part at MIT,

00:10:38.287 --> 00:10:41.226
we're going to be very open

00:10:41.226 --> 00:10:44.078
and essentially let
students make the decision

00:10:44.078 --> 00:10:45.531
as to what they think
will be best for them

00:10:45.531 --> 00:10:47.692
once they have all the information

00:10:47.692 --> 00:10:50.253
as to what our fall program
is going to be like.

00:10:50.253 --> 00:10:53.893
But I don't know how
universal that will be.

00:10:55.442 --> 00:10:58.498
I think, there are a
lot of considerations,

00:10:58.498 --> 00:11:02.029
that colleges and universities will have

00:11:02.029 --> 00:11:06.032
around how many students
that they can allow

00:11:06.032 --> 00:11:08.869
and afford to let defer for a year.

00:11:08.869 --> 00:11:13.869
MIT happens to be in a
relatively privileged position,

00:11:14.550 --> 00:11:18.451
there that, you know,
we can withstand that

00:11:18.451 --> 00:11:20.650
from a financial point of view.

00:11:20.650 --> 00:11:22.610
- Yeah, no, it's, it's fascinating.

00:11:22.610 --> 00:11:24.050
I mean, well we could talk more,

00:11:24.050 --> 00:11:25.270
especially if people ask questions

00:11:25.270 --> 00:11:26.587
and I'm just reminding everyone,

00:11:26.587 --> 00:11:27.510
"If you have questions

00:11:27.510 --> 00:11:28.740
and a lot of questions
have already started

00:11:28.740 --> 00:11:30.640
to come in on a Facebook and YouTube,

00:11:30.640 --> 00:11:31.712
please ask them".

00:11:31.712 --> 00:11:33.540
But I think that's a
whole other dimension of,

00:11:33.540 --> 00:11:35.384
it sounds like the places
like MIT are going to be,

00:11:35.384 --> 00:11:36.900
they're going to be able

00:11:36.900 --> 00:11:38.630
to weather the financial storm of this.

00:11:38.630 --> 00:11:40.280
But there's a lot of colleges

00:11:40.280 --> 00:11:41.465
and universities that were already

00:11:41.465 --> 00:11:43.185
kind of on the fence pre-COVID

00:11:43.185 --> 00:11:45.150
and this might put them over.

00:11:45.150 --> 00:11:47.580
That's a whole other interesting topic.

00:11:47.580 --> 00:11:49.340
One question, just because
there's been so much

00:11:49.340 --> 00:11:50.260
in the news lately.

00:11:50.260 --> 00:11:52.220
I'm sure you are aware, you know,

00:11:52.220 --> 00:11:53.716
the University of California system,

00:11:53.716 --> 00:11:55.900
really kind of stimulated by covid,

00:11:55.900 --> 00:11:57.680
they're like, "Look for
the next couple of years,

00:11:57.680 --> 00:11:58.542
SAT and ACT will be optional".

00:11:58.542 --> 00:12:02.038
And then they've essentially,

00:12:02.038 --> 00:12:03.090
it's not testing optional.

00:12:03.090 --> 00:12:05.520
They've, essentially said
two years, three years out,

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:06.810
they're not going to
consider something like

00:12:06.810 --> 00:12:08.950
the SAT places like University of Chicago.

00:12:08.950 --> 00:12:10.125
We had Jim Nondorf on this show,

00:12:10.125 --> 00:12:13.200
I guess that's what we can call it a show.

00:12:13.200 --> 00:12:14.170
They're testing optional.

00:12:14.170 --> 00:12:15.359
They still have 90% of
their students take the SAT.

00:12:15.359 --> 00:12:17.801
Though I think those are the admitted.

00:12:17.801 --> 00:12:19.790
How are you thinking about it?

00:12:19.790 --> 00:12:20.910
And you know, there are several questions

00:12:20.910 --> 00:12:22.285
from Facebook, you know,

00:12:22.285 --> 00:12:24.150
(mumbles) "What's the process of judging

00:12:24.150 --> 00:12:26.190
an applicant in context?"

00:12:26.190 --> 00:12:27.958
So I think that's, you
know, people hear that.

00:12:27.958 --> 00:12:30.266
So what's your view on
these standardized tests

00:12:30.266 --> 00:12:33.230
and how do you put them in context?

00:12:33.230 --> 00:12:34.824
- Yeah, by the way, big fan of Jim Nondorf

00:12:34.824 --> 00:12:36.470
and I think what he's done

00:12:36.470 --> 00:12:37.500
at the University of Chicago

00:12:37.500 --> 00:12:38.510
has been great in terms of,

00:12:38.510 --> 00:12:40.824
the way they've approached their testing

00:12:40.824 --> 00:12:42.443
because that works for them.

00:12:43.410 --> 00:12:45.569
And I think generally the
conversation around testing,

00:12:45.569 --> 00:12:48.130
nobody loves testing.

00:12:48.130 --> 00:12:49.640
Very few people.

00:12:49.640 --> 00:12:51.430
- I love testing, no, I'm kidding (laughs)

00:12:51.430 --> 00:12:53.670
- Yeah, (laughs) well,
there are people who do,

00:12:53.670 --> 00:12:54.503
I know there are people.

00:12:54.503 --> 00:12:56.182
- Actually I do.

00:12:56.182 --> 00:12:58.410
- And in fact, you know,

00:12:58.410 --> 00:12:59.681
I know when I was in high school,

00:12:59.681 --> 00:13:01.449
I wouldn't necessarily say the SAT

00:13:01.449 --> 00:13:02.980
was the thing that I loved to take,

00:13:02.980 --> 00:13:04.120
but I, you know, went through the,

00:13:04.120 --> 00:13:05.830
all the math competitions

00:13:05.830 --> 00:13:07.209
and loved all of that stuff.

00:13:07.209 --> 00:13:09.190
So, there are students out there who do.

00:13:09.190 --> 00:13:11.244
But by and large, people
don't love testing.

00:13:11.244 --> 00:13:14.930
And I think what people really,

00:13:14.930 --> 00:13:17.923
acknowledges that a test that,

00:13:17.923 --> 00:13:22.923
is so high stakes and therefore stressful

00:13:23.300 --> 00:13:24.206
and pressure filled,

00:13:24.206 --> 00:13:27.541
that determines so much for a student,

00:13:27.541 --> 00:13:28.621
that's not good.

00:13:28.621 --> 00:13:30.210
And I think we would all agree that,

00:13:30.210 --> 00:13:32.087
you know, sitting for one test

00:13:32.087 --> 00:13:33.901
shouldn't determine

00:13:33.901 --> 00:13:37.510
a student's pathway going forward.

00:13:37.510 --> 00:13:39.114
And also people look at the test

00:13:39.114 --> 00:13:42.083
and they see that there
are disparate outcomes.

00:13:44.017 --> 00:13:45.410
So, low income students,

00:13:45.410 --> 00:13:46.493
students of color,

00:13:46.493 --> 00:13:50.658
tend to have lower scores on average.

00:13:50.658 --> 00:13:54.855
And, so, you know, there's a sense that

00:13:54.855 --> 00:13:57.254
there's some unfairness in that.

00:13:57.254 --> 00:14:02.254
So my take on this with
MIT though is that,

00:14:03.530 --> 00:14:08.530
the tests, the SAT, the ACT really are one

00:14:08.700 --> 00:14:13.700
and really the only consistent
measure of some academic,

00:14:13.800 --> 00:14:15.948
output that students can offer.

00:14:15.948 --> 00:14:18.570
We have students applying to us

00:14:18.570 --> 00:14:19.633
from all over the world.

00:14:19.633 --> 00:14:21.220
But even in the United States,

00:14:21.220 --> 00:14:25.700
there's such a diversity
of, K through 12 systems.

00:14:25.700 --> 00:14:27.523
So the high schools in the U.S.

00:14:28.742 --> 00:14:31.500
and we're not familiar with all of them.

00:14:31.500 --> 00:14:33.717
And, so those standardized tests

00:14:33.717 --> 00:14:38.717
are the only real consistent
academic measure that we have.

00:14:39.530 --> 00:14:42.823
And we also find at least the research,

00:14:43.690 --> 00:14:46.248
is that the tests are the best predictor

00:14:46.248 --> 00:14:50.040
of success in our curriculum.

00:14:50.040 --> 00:14:54.700
And MIT, right from day one,

00:14:54.700 --> 00:14:57.150
is a pretty challenging place

00:14:58.130 --> 00:14:59.800
and we wanna make sure that

00:14:59.800 --> 00:15:00.948
any student that we admit

00:15:00.948 --> 00:15:03.229
is going to be academically ready

00:15:03.229 --> 00:15:05.457
and prepared to do that work

00:15:05.457 --> 00:15:07.411
regardless of where they come from.

00:15:07.411 --> 00:15:11.620
And the tests give us that confidence

00:15:11.620 --> 00:15:15.840
to be able to admit students knowing that

00:15:15.840 --> 00:15:17.662
they're gonna be successful.

00:15:17.662 --> 00:15:20.560
And the tests give us that confidence

00:15:20.560 --> 00:15:23.708
in ways that grades alone do not.

00:15:23.708 --> 00:15:28.610
And so, for us, the tests play a role.

00:15:28.610 --> 00:15:31.087
They are not the only
thing that we consider.

00:15:31.087 --> 00:15:34.895
We also are fully aware,
as I mentioned earlier,

00:15:34.895 --> 00:15:38.680
that there are, you know, different groups

00:15:38.680 --> 00:15:41.137
that will perform differently
based on resources.

00:15:41.137 --> 00:15:45.650
And in fact, you know, family income

00:15:45.650 --> 00:15:48.250
is maybe the best predictor

00:15:48.250 --> 00:15:51.202
of a student's SAT score.

00:15:51.202 --> 00:15:54.642
All that said, we're aware of that

00:15:54.642 --> 00:15:57.550
and we're aware of that
in the admissions process.

00:15:57.550 --> 00:16:00.230
And so you asked a question

00:16:00.230 --> 00:16:01.708
or one of our listeners
asked a question about,

00:16:01.708 --> 00:16:04.975
what does it mean to, do
college admissions in context?

00:16:04.975 --> 00:16:07.385
And what that means is

00:16:07.385 --> 00:16:10.623
really understanding the environment,

00:16:10.623 --> 00:16:12.390
the opportunities

00:16:12.390 --> 00:16:15.513
and the resources that students have had,

00:16:17.200 --> 00:16:19.810
and making an evaluation of their talent

00:16:19.810 --> 00:16:21.684
and their potential based on

00:16:21.684 --> 00:16:23.400
where they come from

00:16:23.400 --> 00:16:25.506
and how they've performed
in their context.

00:16:25.506 --> 00:16:27.469
You know, one way to think about this is,

00:16:27.469 --> 00:16:32.469
you know, imagine two runners racing

00:16:33.170 --> 00:16:36.350
and people think that,
"Okay, that's fair".

00:16:36.350 --> 00:16:39.300
You line up two people
and you let them run

00:16:39.300 --> 00:16:42.850
and whoever gets to the finish line first,

00:16:42.850 --> 00:16:45.030
is a faster runner.

00:16:45.030 --> 00:16:48.073
But you also have to
consider the fact that,

00:16:49.081 --> 00:16:50.952
the two runners may be running

00:16:50.952 --> 00:16:52.794
under different conditions.

00:16:52.794 --> 00:16:55.120
One may be running into a headwind

00:16:55.120 --> 00:16:56.858
while the other has a
really strong tailwind.

00:16:56.858 --> 00:17:00.350
And if you really wanna judge the talent

00:17:00.350 --> 00:17:02.560
and the potential of those runners,

00:17:02.560 --> 00:17:05.194
you have to understand the environment,

00:17:05.194 --> 00:17:07.483
the headwinds, the
tailwinds that they may have

00:17:07.483 --> 00:17:10.600
in order to make those fair assessments.

00:17:10.600 --> 00:17:11.626
So that's what it means

00:17:11.626 --> 00:17:14.973
to do admissions in
context is understanding,

00:17:15.811 --> 00:17:17.480
the environment

00:17:17.480 --> 00:17:20.182
or the headwinds that
students have to face.

00:17:20.182 --> 00:17:21.430
- It makes a ton of sense.

00:17:21.430 --> 00:17:22.509
And just more tactically short term

00:17:22.509 --> 00:17:25.152
because some of the testing
administration dates

00:17:25.152 --> 00:17:26.700
have to be pushed back.

00:17:26.700 --> 00:17:28.237
Some of the tests got canceled there.

00:17:28.237 --> 00:17:29.190
"How are you all thinking?"

00:17:29.190 --> 00:17:31.229
This is a question,
Anaf Swat from YouTube.

00:17:31.229 --> 00:17:34.221
"What about SAT and ACT for
this coming admission cycle?

00:17:34.221 --> 00:17:37.377
How are you all thinking about that?"

00:17:38.230 --> 00:17:40.700
- Well, we know that it's possible

00:17:40.700 --> 00:17:42.231
that students may not be able to,

00:17:42.231 --> 00:17:44.780
have access to tests.

00:17:44.780 --> 00:17:45.613
That's possible

00:17:45.613 --> 00:17:48.730
and if that's true, we'll adjust our,

00:17:48.730 --> 00:17:49.759
admissions process.

00:17:49.759 --> 00:17:53.550
Admissions process takes in
quite a number of inputs.

00:17:53.550 --> 00:17:56.740
So the test is just one of many,

00:17:56.740 --> 00:17:59.010
many inputs that we have.

00:17:59.010 --> 00:18:01.130
And, we like to have it

00:18:01.130 --> 00:18:02.580
because as I mentioned earlier,

00:18:02.580 --> 00:18:03.748
it's helpful for us,

00:18:03.748 --> 00:18:05.180
but if we don't have it,

00:18:05.180 --> 00:18:06.388
we can still make decisions using

00:18:06.388 --> 00:18:08.790
the best information that we have

00:18:08.790 --> 00:18:11.370
from the rest of the application.

00:18:11.370 --> 00:18:15.480
And, you know, we haven't made

00:18:15.480 --> 00:18:17.203
or set or changed our policies

00:18:17.203 --> 00:18:19.346
for next year as a number of schools have,

00:18:19.346 --> 00:18:22.010
quite a number of schools have said

00:18:22.010 --> 00:18:23.700
that they are from next year,

00:18:23.700 --> 00:18:25.135
going to be test optional

00:18:25.135 --> 00:18:27.640
in anticipation of the fact that

00:18:27.640 --> 00:18:30.476
many students won't
have access to the tests

00:18:30.476 --> 00:18:31.985
and that may come to pass.

00:18:31.985 --> 00:18:35.320
But we're waiting just to see

00:18:35.320 --> 00:18:37.080
how things develop in the fall

00:18:37.080 --> 00:18:38.810
in the hopes that there will be

00:18:38.810 --> 00:18:40.630
some ability for students

00:18:40.630 --> 00:18:43.479
to be able to take those tests.

00:18:43.479 --> 00:18:44.770
- It makes sense.

00:18:44.770 --> 00:18:45.850
And you know, one question,

00:18:45.850 --> 00:18:47.775
this is, from YouTube Lauren Crowe,

00:18:47.775 --> 00:18:49.228
"Does MIT measure the
passion a student has

00:18:49.228 --> 00:18:51.360
in the area they are applying for?"

00:18:51.360 --> 00:18:52.367
And I'll just add to that,

00:18:52.367 --> 00:18:54.130
you know, there's kind
of these two schools

00:18:54.130 --> 00:18:55.456
of thought and admissions.

00:18:55.456 --> 00:18:57.670
One is, kind of what you hear,

00:18:57.670 --> 00:18:58.674
word of mouth and a lot of,

00:18:58.674 --> 00:19:01.381
especially, I would say
competitive high schools

00:19:01.381 --> 00:19:03.555
where there seems to be
institutional knowledge,

00:19:03.555 --> 00:19:04.920
but they're, you know,

00:19:04.920 --> 00:19:07.230
there's a lot of kids
joining tons of clubs,

00:19:07.230 --> 00:19:09.996
taking a ton of AP courses.

00:19:09.996 --> 00:19:11.954
You know, they might be taking

00:19:11.954 --> 00:19:14.430
the SAT or the ACT three, four,

00:19:14.430 --> 00:19:15.740
you know, I've heard horror stories,

00:19:15.740 --> 00:19:17.313
even more times than that.

00:19:17.313 --> 00:19:19.270
you know, and there's this kind

00:19:19.270 --> 00:19:20.940
of the stereotypical application

00:19:20.940 --> 00:19:21.940
and these kids are kind of

00:19:21.940 --> 00:19:23.460
in this pressure cooker environment,

00:19:23.460 --> 00:19:24.470
they're kind of check boxing.

00:19:24.470 --> 00:19:25.930
That's one school of thought.

00:19:25.930 --> 00:19:27.660
But then whenever you talk
to an admissions officer,

00:19:27.660 --> 00:19:28.690
there's like, "No, no, no, no,

00:19:28.690 --> 00:19:30.050
that's missing the point.

00:19:30.050 --> 00:19:30.883
We care about,

00:19:30.883 --> 00:19:32.355
you know, your whole being.

00:19:32.355 --> 00:19:34.338
We wanna see people who
have a passion in one

00:19:34.338 --> 00:19:37.103
or a few areas who have this potential".

00:19:37.103 --> 00:19:39.960
So you know, and then I think the people

00:19:39.960 --> 00:19:41.317
that first school thought say,

00:19:41.317 --> 00:19:42.650
"Oh, well they just say that,

00:19:42.650 --> 00:19:44.300
you know, that's kind of
the correct thing to say,

00:19:44.300 --> 00:19:45.922
but they really want us gunners".

00:19:45.922 --> 00:19:49.380
So, clarify, what's the truth?

00:19:49.380 --> 00:19:51.152
- Yeah, so the truth really is

00:19:51.152 --> 00:19:54.179
the latter that we really, we...

00:19:54.179 --> 00:19:56.940
I'm gonna tell you what
all admissions officers

00:19:56.940 --> 00:20:00.171
have been telling you for years and--

00:20:00.171 --> 00:20:01.170
- Tell us the truth.

00:20:01.170 --> 00:20:02.050
- That is the truth

00:20:02.050 --> 00:20:03.169
- No (laughing)

00:20:03.169 --> 00:20:04.420
- And the reason why it gets confused...

00:20:04.420 --> 00:20:06.021
Well, here's the reason
why it gets confused,

00:20:06.021 --> 00:20:11.021
is because, students,
parents make judgements

00:20:12.132 --> 00:20:14.735
on very few data points

00:20:14.735 --> 00:20:18.988
and in fact data points
that they don't even,

00:20:18.988 --> 00:20:22.033
know fully.

00:20:22.033 --> 00:20:27.033
They see who gets admitted to our schools

00:20:27.510 --> 00:20:29.950
and they make assumptions about

00:20:29.950 --> 00:20:32.653
why they may have been
admitted to our schools.

00:20:33.840 --> 00:20:37.069
It's never the volume

00:20:37.069 --> 00:20:39.730
or the quantity,

00:20:39.730 --> 00:20:42.693
of what a student does.

00:20:42.693 --> 00:20:43.546
It's never that.

00:20:43.546 --> 00:20:47.210
It's always, you know,

00:20:47.210 --> 00:20:49.620
who they are as a person, their talent,

00:20:49.620 --> 00:20:52.083
really their match with our mission.

00:20:53.520 --> 00:20:54.431
You know, for MIT, of course,

00:20:54.431 --> 00:20:56.490
we're a school that is centered

00:20:56.490 --> 00:20:58.500
on science and technology.

00:20:58.500 --> 00:21:01.020
So we like to see that interest.

00:21:01.020 --> 00:21:02.490
But we're also a place that's trying

00:21:02.490 --> 00:21:03.990
to make the world a better place.

00:21:03.990 --> 00:21:06.130
In fact, right in our mission statement

00:21:06.130 --> 00:21:07.600
to apply science, technology

00:21:07.600 --> 00:21:09.139
and other areas of scholarship,

00:21:09.139 --> 00:21:12.830
to best serve the nation in the world,

00:21:12.830 --> 00:21:14.770
in the 21st century.

00:21:14.770 --> 00:21:17.540
And we are looking for students

00:21:17.540 --> 00:21:18.860
who we think are gonna resonate

00:21:18.860 --> 00:21:20.410
with that mission.

00:21:20.410 --> 00:21:23.560
And if all you are is cranking away,

00:21:23.560 --> 00:21:25.660
loading up on activities

00:21:25.660 --> 00:21:26.596
and advanced classes

00:21:26.596 --> 00:21:27.831
and all the rest of it,

00:21:27.831 --> 00:21:30.580
it's very hard in fact.

00:21:30.580 --> 00:21:31.927
I think students do themselves

00:21:31.927 --> 00:21:33.617
a disservice when they do that

00:21:33.617 --> 00:21:35.852
because they don't leave themselves time

00:21:35.852 --> 00:21:38.970
to cultivate their interests

00:21:38.970 --> 00:21:42.490
and to think about how
they can make an impact

00:21:42.490 --> 00:21:43.610
on their communities.

00:21:43.610 --> 00:21:44.917
We actually pay attention to that.

00:21:44.917 --> 00:21:47.060
You know, how much are students helping

00:21:47.060 --> 00:21:48.223
the people around them?

00:21:49.241 --> 00:21:52.764
And I think if students are just flat out

00:21:52.764 --> 00:21:55.320
and on overload,

00:21:55.320 --> 00:21:57.514
they really do themselves a disservice

00:21:57.514 --> 00:22:01.320
because they can't put the time

00:22:01.320 --> 00:22:04.040
and the mental energy into the things

00:22:04.040 --> 00:22:06.350
that really are interesting to them

00:22:06.350 --> 00:22:08.998
and develop those talents more fully.

00:22:08.998 --> 00:22:11.160
- And how do you discern that?

00:22:11.160 --> 00:22:12.152
I mean, to the question from Lauren,

00:22:12.152 --> 00:22:14.376
you know, how can you separate

00:22:14.376 --> 00:22:16.770
or you know, folks who are maybe

00:22:16.770 --> 00:22:17.975
even very sophisticated at applying,

00:22:17.975 --> 00:22:19.330
you know, maybe,

00:22:19.330 --> 00:22:20.950
there's obviously cases of consultants,

00:22:20.950 --> 00:22:22.633
some of which obviously
famously had been illegal,

00:22:22.633 --> 00:22:24.841
where, you know, these
perfect applications

00:22:24.841 --> 00:22:27.940
versus kids who are truly authentic

00:22:27.940 --> 00:22:28.840
about those passions,

00:22:28.840 --> 00:22:29.820
and they would have had those passions

00:22:29.820 --> 00:22:30.960
even if they weren't trying to get

00:22:30.960 --> 00:22:32.232
into a selective university?

00:22:32.232 --> 00:22:34.985
- Right, so again, I mentioned earlier,

00:22:34.985 --> 00:22:37.688
we get inputs from a range of places.

00:22:37.688 --> 00:22:38.833
So on the academic side,

00:22:38.833 --> 00:22:40.712
the end of the test scores, the grades.

00:22:40.712 --> 00:22:42.610
And there are other ways that students

00:22:42.610 --> 00:22:45.575
can demonstrate academic
talent and ability.

00:22:45.575 --> 00:22:47.641
We ask students to write essays,

00:22:47.641 --> 00:22:50.376
to tell those about themselves

00:22:50.376 --> 00:22:53.098
and actually I think while, yes,

00:22:53.098 --> 00:22:54.969
there are consultants out there

00:22:54.969 --> 00:22:56.406
and students can basically

00:22:56.406 --> 00:22:57.770
tell us anything they want to,

00:22:57.770 --> 00:23:01.597
we generally trust that students are,

00:23:01.597 --> 00:23:02.980
speaking from the heart

00:23:02.980 --> 00:23:04.650
when they write those essays.

00:23:04.650 --> 00:23:06.519
But we also get letters
of recommendation from,

00:23:06.519 --> 00:23:11.220
teachers and counselors in schools.

00:23:11.220 --> 00:23:12.713
And those have to align.

00:23:13.900 --> 00:23:15.440
And teachers and counselors,

00:23:15.440 --> 00:23:18.660
often do a pretty good job at describing

00:23:18.660 --> 00:23:19.740
who the students are,

00:23:19.740 --> 00:23:23.170
and we make sure there's
some alignment there.

00:23:23.170 --> 00:23:24.510
We also offer an interview,

00:23:24.510 --> 00:23:26.870
although that's, not every student

00:23:26.870 --> 00:23:27.763
has access to the interview,

00:23:27.763 --> 00:23:29.043
but many, many do.

00:23:29.043 --> 00:23:30.785
And the interview can shed more light,

00:23:30.785 --> 00:23:34.710
in ways that are harder to gain.

00:23:34.710 --> 00:23:36.623
And, we think that,

00:23:38.050 --> 00:23:38.975
we are able to discern

00:23:38.975 --> 00:23:42.283
when students are being authentic.

00:23:43.130 --> 00:23:47.403
You know, it's so interesting to me that,

00:23:48.330 --> 00:23:50.223
after we admit students in a given cycle

00:23:50.223 --> 00:23:52.880
and I have the opportunity
to meet with them

00:23:52.880 --> 00:23:54.099
when they first come on campus,

00:23:54.099 --> 00:23:58.120
so many of them come up to me

00:23:58.120 --> 00:24:02.243
and they ask me if they
were admitted by mistake

00:24:02.243 --> 00:24:07.243
or they feel like, how is
it that they got admitted?

00:24:08.120 --> 00:24:11.680
And, you know, to me that
says a couple of things.

00:24:11.680 --> 00:24:14.875
Number one, I mean, I
love the MIT students

00:24:14.875 --> 00:24:17.299
and there's a real humbleness,

00:24:17.299 --> 00:24:19.192
to the group that, you know,

00:24:19.192 --> 00:24:22.490
they don't have this overinflated view

00:24:22.490 --> 00:24:23.323
of who they are.

00:24:23.323 --> 00:24:25.233
But I think also they recognize that,

00:24:25.233 --> 00:24:29.273
they're just regular students.

00:24:29.273 --> 00:24:30.625
They're not perfect.

00:24:30.625 --> 00:24:32.523
They see their own flaws.

00:24:33.600 --> 00:24:37.299
And we don't admit perfect people.

00:24:37.299 --> 00:24:39.603
They don't exist out there.

00:24:40.666 --> 00:24:43.759
Everyone has flaws and that's fine.

00:24:43.759 --> 00:24:47.800
And we're not looking for
that perfect applicant,

00:24:47.800 --> 00:24:48.710
that perfect student

00:24:48.710 --> 00:24:50.410
because they don't exist out there.

00:24:50.410 --> 00:24:52.350
And, you know,

00:24:52.350 --> 00:24:53.790
I think that if students

00:24:53.790 --> 00:24:55.114
could relax a little bit more

00:24:55.114 --> 00:25:00.080
and just focus on doing the things

00:25:00.080 --> 00:25:02.030
that are most interesting to them

00:25:02.030 --> 00:25:03.060
with as much energy

00:25:03.060 --> 00:25:05.023
and enthusiasm as they can,

00:25:05.023 --> 00:25:08.380
and not to forget that
they are family members

00:25:08.380 --> 00:25:09.898
and community members

00:25:09.898 --> 00:25:11.416
and they need to pay attention

00:25:11.416 --> 00:25:13.892
to being good contributing members,

00:25:13.892 --> 00:25:16.019
to their family and their communities,

00:25:16.019 --> 00:25:19.210
in pursuit of something else

00:25:19.210 --> 00:25:20.699
they can't give that piece of it up.

00:25:20.699 --> 00:25:23.145
Because we're looking for all of that.

00:25:23.145 --> 00:25:25.209
And I think if students

00:25:25.209 --> 00:25:26.876
could just relax a little bit

00:25:26.876 --> 00:25:29.228
and not try to make themselves

00:25:29.228 --> 00:25:30.930
into the perfect applicant,

00:25:30.930 --> 00:25:33.541
but just to be the best person they can be

00:25:33.541 --> 00:25:38.541
and let the applications flow from that,

00:25:39.044 --> 00:25:41.670
I think students would have a much

00:25:41.670 --> 00:25:42.822
better experience in high school

00:25:42.822 --> 00:25:44.342
and ultimately the world

00:25:44.342 --> 00:25:46.103
would be a better place.

00:25:47.302 --> 00:25:49.050
- You know, and for anyone
who doesn't believe,

00:25:49.050 --> 00:25:49.883
Stu, you know,

00:25:49.883 --> 00:25:50.945
I'll even use my own personal example,

00:25:50.945 --> 00:25:52.892
as kind of a case in point,

00:25:52.892 --> 00:25:56.131
where you know, many people know that

00:25:56.131 --> 00:25:58.240
I was an undergrad at MIT

00:25:58.240 --> 00:25:59.900
and you know,

00:25:59.900 --> 00:26:00.733
I went to a school

00:26:00.733 --> 00:26:02.749
that had never sent anyone to MIT.

00:26:02.749 --> 00:26:04.995
I was somewhat naive about the process,

00:26:04.995 --> 00:26:07.620
but I did just kind of very authentically

00:26:07.620 --> 00:26:08.840
put my best foot forward.

00:26:08.840 --> 00:26:10.130
It looks like I'm
clipping up a little bit.

00:26:10.130 --> 00:26:12.430
My internet might be
acting a little bit shady.

00:26:12.430 --> 00:26:15.122
My apologies, but that, you know,

00:26:15.122 --> 00:26:16.757
I remember after I had applied,

00:26:16.757 --> 00:26:18.880
I had met some kids who went

00:26:18.880 --> 00:26:21.146
to some of the fancy schools
at a math competition

00:26:21.146 --> 00:26:23.060
and they almost laughed at me

00:26:23.060 --> 00:26:24.920
when they found out that
I was applying to MIT

00:26:24.920 --> 00:26:26.580
cause my school was just
kind of a completely

00:26:26.580 --> 00:26:29.213
off the radar, type of place.

00:26:29.213 --> 00:26:32.720
And I was actually frankly
very intimidated by them

00:26:32.720 --> 00:26:33.897
because when they told me how many

00:26:33.897 --> 00:26:35.440
who had applied from

00:26:35.440 --> 00:26:37.034
their very fancy school in the past

00:26:37.034 --> 00:26:38.452
and maybe hadn't got in,

00:26:38.452 --> 00:26:40.350
I was like, "Oh!" (laughs)

00:26:40.350 --> 00:26:42.020
I guess I was naive about this process.

00:26:42.020 --> 00:26:45.803
But, only later when I did get in

00:26:45.803 --> 00:26:47.780
and I kind of reflected about the process

00:26:47.780 --> 00:26:48.772
is exactly what Stu was describing.

00:26:48.772 --> 00:26:52.640
A lot of, you know, a lot
of those other applicants

00:26:52.640 --> 00:26:54.450
were kind of engineered applicants.

00:26:54.450 --> 00:26:56.606
And I think, whoever
your predecessors were,

00:26:56.606 --> 00:26:58.983
Stu, you know, kind of spotted that,

00:26:58.983 --> 00:27:00.610
while they were looking for, you know,

00:27:00.610 --> 00:27:01.940
I don't wanna make it
sound like they were,

00:27:01.940 --> 00:27:03.530
you know, they did a great
decision admitting me.

00:27:03.530 --> 00:27:05.190
I wouldn't even ask y'all
whatever the mistake,

00:27:05.190 --> 00:27:06.875
I don't want It (laughing)

00:27:06.875 --> 00:27:10.434
But I do see that for real,

00:27:10.434 --> 00:27:12.940
that people's perceptions before a college

00:27:12.940 --> 00:27:14.630
and then what you see
there, what admissions...

00:27:14.630 --> 00:27:17.156
The admissions officers
are telling the reality.

00:27:17.156 --> 00:27:20.750
Now, a related question to that, you know,

00:27:20.750 --> 00:27:21.583
obviously I had a ...

00:27:21.583 --> 00:27:23.224
If someone asks me what
was my experience at MIT?

00:27:23.224 --> 00:27:24.958
"Look, I had a wonderful
experience at MIT".

00:27:24.958 --> 00:27:28.075
I met my wife there, my
many, my best friends,

00:27:28.075 --> 00:27:29.940
some of my best memories.

00:27:29.940 --> 00:27:31.560
I still have very positive dreams.

00:27:31.560 --> 00:27:32.840
Still, I have times that I'm with my,

00:27:32.840 --> 00:27:34.520
you know, sometimes I
forgot to drop a class

00:27:34.520 --> 00:27:37.090
and that kind of as a stressful dream.

00:27:37.090 --> 00:27:38.722
But I had very positive experience there,

00:27:38.722 --> 00:27:40.537
so I couldn't endorse it more highly.

00:27:40.537 --> 00:27:42.760
But what do you say to folks, you know,

00:27:42.760 --> 00:27:44.480
there's also this sense of like,

00:27:44.480 --> 00:27:45.347
name brandism,

00:27:45.347 --> 00:27:46.580
"Oh, I've got to go to an MIT,

00:27:46.580 --> 00:27:47.460
I've gotta go to a Harvard,

00:27:47.460 --> 00:27:48.830
I've gotta go to a Stanford".

00:27:48.830 --> 00:27:50.072
Otherwise, you know,

00:27:50.072 --> 00:27:51.521
It's the end of the world,

00:27:51.521 --> 00:27:53.721
what do you tell that to families

00:27:53.721 --> 00:27:57.250
or students who are feeling
that type of anxiety?

00:27:57.250 --> 00:28:00.929
- Yeah, again, this is where I feel like,

00:28:00.929 --> 00:28:04.214
students would do well
to just think about,

00:28:04.214 --> 00:28:05.990
not think of themselves

00:28:05.990 --> 00:28:07.609
as college applicants first,

00:28:07.609 --> 00:28:10.280
but just to think of
themselves as students

00:28:10.280 --> 00:28:11.374
and community members first

00:28:11.374 --> 00:28:15.062
and then let the college
admissions process,

00:28:15.062 --> 00:28:16.656
fall where it may,

00:28:16.656 --> 00:28:19.720
because the reality is,

00:28:19.720 --> 00:28:23.034
that, you know, if MIT is a great place,

00:28:23.034 --> 00:28:28.034
it's not a great place because you know,

00:28:29.514 --> 00:28:32.595
from up on high it's been
deemed that, you know,

00:28:32.595 --> 00:28:34.343
MIT is this magical place.

00:28:34.343 --> 00:28:36.040
It's only a great place

00:28:36.040 --> 00:28:38.337
because the people that are there,

00:28:38.337 --> 00:28:41.927
and that the students and the faculty

00:28:41.927 --> 00:28:43.749
and the staff that are there,

00:28:43.749 --> 00:28:44.836
they make it so,

00:28:44.836 --> 00:28:47.757
and so if you're a great student,

00:28:47.757 --> 00:28:49.273
you can go anywhere

00:28:49.273 --> 00:28:52.160
and have a great experience

00:28:52.160 --> 00:28:55.683
and make that place just
as magical and great.

00:28:55.683 --> 00:29:00.250
It's really true that, you know,

00:29:00.250 --> 00:29:01.090
college is what you make it.

00:29:01.090 --> 00:29:03.190
And any college in the U.S.

00:29:03.190 --> 00:29:06.380
has more opportunity than any one student

00:29:06.380 --> 00:29:07.987
can possibly take advantage of.

00:29:07.987 --> 00:29:11.069
And, so students can thrive

00:29:11.069 --> 00:29:13.580
wherever they go to school

00:29:13.580 --> 00:29:15.180
they find a place that they feel like

00:29:15.180 --> 00:29:16.013
really fits the style

00:29:16.013 --> 00:29:17.280
and the environment

00:29:17.280 --> 00:29:18.433
that they wanna learn.

00:29:19.900 --> 00:29:22.028
And any student who takes advantage

00:29:22.028 --> 00:29:24.969
at any college is gonna be successful

00:29:24.969 --> 00:29:28.173
and you don't need to go to an MIT,

00:29:29.330 --> 00:29:32.182
or you know, another deemed brand college.

00:29:32.182 --> 00:29:35.840
You just have to cultivate yourself

00:29:35.840 --> 00:29:37.230
and someone who's gonna
engage wherever you go.

00:29:37.230 --> 00:29:41.372
And that's how you
build a successful life.

00:29:41.372 --> 00:29:43.730
- That completely resonates.

00:29:43.730 --> 00:29:44.710
A lot of what we've talked about

00:29:44.710 --> 00:29:45.640
at this live stream before

00:29:45.640 --> 00:29:47.250
is kind of what you put out,

00:29:47.250 --> 00:29:48.451
the energy you put out is kind of

00:29:48.451 --> 00:29:49.519
what you experience.

00:29:49.519 --> 00:29:51.305
And I even gave the example,

00:29:51.305 --> 00:29:53.240
I think I've given this on
this live stream before,

00:29:53.240 --> 00:29:54.073
when I was in business school,

00:29:54.073 --> 00:29:56.560
you know, I went with some stereotypes

00:29:56.560 --> 00:29:57.393
of business school,

00:29:57.393 --> 00:29:58.780
but then when I got there, you know,

00:29:58.780 --> 00:30:00.170
I was kind of open minded about things.

00:30:00.170 --> 00:30:01.067
I was very vulnerable, open to folks

00:30:01.067 --> 00:30:02.722
and I was like, "Oh,
they're just like that,

00:30:02.722 --> 00:30:03.920
they're very open

00:30:03.920 --> 00:30:04.770
and vulnerable to me

00:30:04.770 --> 00:30:06.467
and they care more about friendships

00:30:06.467 --> 00:30:07.300
than you know,

00:30:07.300 --> 00:30:08.460
in some ways being competitive".

00:30:08.460 --> 00:30:09.849
But then I remember
meeting someone who's like,

00:30:09.849 --> 00:30:11.453
"You know, can you believe this place?

00:30:11.453 --> 00:30:12.741
How competitive everyone is?

00:30:12.741 --> 00:30:14.073
I can't trust anyone here" (laughs)

00:30:14.073 --> 00:30:17.540
I was just like, "Are you
on in a different universe?

00:30:17.540 --> 00:30:18.875
We're sharing the same air".

00:30:18.875 --> 00:30:21.310
But I think, you know, I didn't tell him,

00:30:21.310 --> 00:30:23.090
but I was like, "Yeah, I
think you're projecting that".

00:30:23.090 --> 00:30:26.300
So (laughs) completely I agree.

00:30:26.300 --> 00:30:27.280
You can go anywhere

00:30:27.280 --> 00:30:29.120
and have an amazing experience

00:30:29.120 --> 00:30:30.390
and you can go anywhere

00:30:30.390 --> 00:30:31.510
and have a horrible one.

00:30:31.510 --> 00:30:32.830
And it's not always your fault.

00:30:32.830 --> 00:30:34.000
Obviously, like I'm not gonna say

00:30:34.000 --> 00:30:35.223
like everything that happens to you is,

00:30:35.223 --> 00:30:36.366
like what you're projecting,

00:30:36.366 --> 00:30:37.401
but a lot of that,

00:30:37.401 --> 00:30:39.369
whatever the context is,

00:30:39.369 --> 00:30:41.002
can often be shaped

00:30:41.002 --> 00:30:42.700
by what you're projecting into it.

00:30:42.700 --> 00:30:44.760
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:30:44.760 --> 00:30:47.540
- So, Stu, any final,
my internet connection

00:30:47.540 --> 00:30:48.373
looks like...

00:30:48.373 --> 00:30:49.206
My apologies.

00:30:49.206 --> 00:30:50.190
It looks like it's falling apart

00:30:50.190 --> 00:30:51.210
and I also have a toothache,

00:30:51.210 --> 00:30:52.465
I feel like blaming my tooth on the...

00:30:52.465 --> 00:30:53.734
(laughs)

00:30:53.734 --> 00:30:55.192
Somehow (mumbles) to make
my whole life pretty...

00:30:55.192 --> 00:30:56.989
I shouldn't project onto my tooth.

00:30:56.989 --> 00:30:59.401
I should have an open mind rather.

00:30:59.401 --> 00:31:00.234
(laughs)

00:31:00.234 --> 00:31:01.360
Teaching me something.

00:31:01.360 --> 00:31:02.200
But Stu, any final thoughts?

00:31:02.200 --> 00:31:04.163
You know, let me just ask,

00:31:04.163 --> 00:31:05.536
I'll finish with a big question.

00:31:05.536 --> 00:31:06.830
What do you think are really gonna be

00:31:06.830 --> 00:31:10.040
the lasting implications on admissions,

00:31:10.040 --> 00:31:11.850
on how people view college

00:31:11.850 --> 00:31:13.550
and how colleges might change

00:31:13.550 --> 00:31:15.483
because of all of this?

00:31:16.330 --> 00:31:17.364
- Well, I think we're learning a lot.

00:31:17.364 --> 00:31:19.335
We're forced to learn a lot

00:31:19.335 --> 00:31:23.670
cause we're doing things
in new mode right now.

00:31:23.670 --> 00:31:26.984
And I think anytime you're
forced to make changes,

00:31:26.984 --> 00:31:28.350
you learn a lot.

00:31:28.350 --> 00:31:29.700
And I think ultimately

00:31:29.700 --> 00:31:32.825
when we get back to
whatever that new normal is,

00:31:32.825 --> 00:31:36.527
I hope it's not exactly
the way it was in the past.

00:31:36.527 --> 00:31:39.840
I hope there are things
that we can take with us,

00:31:39.840 --> 00:31:41.230
that go forward.

00:31:41.230 --> 00:31:42.063
You know, think about,

00:31:42.063 --> 00:31:45.739
certainly online learning is one,

00:31:45.739 --> 00:31:48.142
a way that I think we're gonna

00:31:48.142 --> 00:31:49.440
learn a lot about it

00:31:49.440 --> 00:31:52.637
and perhaps take a hybrid learning

00:31:52.637 --> 00:31:54.230
into the future as well.

00:31:54.230 --> 00:31:55.891
I mean, Khan Academy, a great example of

00:31:55.891 --> 00:31:59.781
an exceptional online learning experience

00:31:59.781 --> 00:32:03.860
and, so it is possible to do that.

00:32:03.860 --> 00:32:06.813
And I think if we can take that forward

00:32:06.813 --> 00:32:08.434
into the universities,

00:32:08.434 --> 00:32:11.633
we can understand what matters most,

00:32:12.710 --> 00:32:14.190
and optimize what matters most.

00:32:14.190 --> 00:32:16.322
And I guess that's
really my final thought,

00:32:16.322 --> 00:32:18.783
is, you know, in the pandemic,

00:32:21.160 --> 00:32:22.916
colleges and universities and students,

00:32:22.916 --> 00:32:24.367
we're all thinking about

00:32:24.367 --> 00:32:26.316
how to take care of ourselves

00:32:26.316 --> 00:32:27.618
and the people around us

00:32:27.618 --> 00:32:30.243
as the absolute top priority.

00:32:31.160 --> 00:32:33.870
And I hope that when we do get back

00:32:33.870 --> 00:32:35.700
to the other side of this pandemic,

00:32:35.700 --> 00:32:37.613
that those values remain,

00:32:37.613 --> 00:32:39.333
that we think about,

00:32:39.333 --> 00:32:41.410
how can we take care of ourselves

00:32:41.410 --> 00:32:43.030
and the people around us

00:32:43.030 --> 00:32:45.020
as a top priority

00:32:45.020 --> 00:32:46.200
and not get caught up?

00:32:46.200 --> 00:32:48.650
We call about getting caught up

00:32:48.650 --> 00:32:51.152
in engineering as the,

00:32:51.152 --> 00:32:54.040
engineering oneself as
the perfect applicant

00:32:54.040 --> 00:32:55.376
and all these other things

00:32:55.376 --> 00:32:58.197
that I've just sort of
noise in the system.

00:32:58.197 --> 00:33:00.572
And I hope we can all remember really,

00:33:00.572 --> 00:33:03.160
what matters most

00:33:03.160 --> 00:33:05.880
and take that with us,

00:33:05.880 --> 00:33:08.230
when we get to the other
side of this pandemic.

00:33:09.400 --> 00:33:10.233
- Now, thanks for that.

00:33:10.233 --> 00:33:11.414
That's super helpful and Stu thanks for,

00:33:11.414 --> 00:33:13.640
thank you for being part of this.

00:33:13.640 --> 00:33:15.470
And I'm sure that there's

00:33:15.470 --> 00:33:17.070
a lot of tough issues that y'all

00:33:17.070 --> 00:33:17.903
are working through,

00:33:17.903 --> 00:33:18.736
but it sounds like you'll have

00:33:18.736 --> 00:33:19.970
a good handle on it.

00:33:19.970 --> 00:33:21.400
So thanks for joining.

00:33:21.400 --> 00:33:22.233
- Great.

00:33:22.233 --> 00:33:23.066
Thanks Sal, great to see you

00:33:23.066 --> 00:33:25.425
and appreciate everyone
who's watching as well.

00:33:25.425 --> 00:33:28.063
Good luck to everyone, be well.

00:33:30.590 --> 00:33:31.423
- So thanks everyone

00:33:31.423 --> 00:33:33.060
for joining this live stream.

00:33:33.060 --> 00:33:33.893
My apologies.

00:33:33.893 --> 00:33:35.020
My internet is breaking up

00:33:35.020 --> 00:33:36.749
and if I haven't been
at the top of my game,

00:33:36.749 --> 00:33:39.919
I'm having a horrible toothache right now.

00:33:39.919 --> 00:33:43.316
And so I might have to do some covid safe,

00:33:43.316 --> 00:33:45.278
emergency dental, type of thing.

00:33:45.278 --> 00:33:48.084
So wish me luck (laughing)

00:33:48.084 --> 00:33:50.070
But you know,

00:33:50.070 --> 00:33:51.360
I thought it was a
fascinating conversation.

00:33:51.360 --> 00:33:52.750
I think, you know, the biggest takeaway

00:33:52.750 --> 00:33:54.467
I think from Stu is,

00:33:54.467 --> 00:33:56.054
and maybe the covid crisis is,

00:33:56.054 --> 00:33:57.540
there's a lot of things that we take

00:33:57.540 --> 00:33:58.840
very, very seriously in our life

00:33:58.840 --> 00:34:00.134
and it creates kind of
this artificial stress

00:34:00.134 --> 00:34:01.545
in our minds that only humans,

00:34:01.545 --> 00:34:04.710
you know, other mammals don't think about,

00:34:04.710 --> 00:34:06.168
like, "Oh, if I have to go to this college

00:34:06.168 --> 00:34:07.417
and I need this score and all that".

00:34:07.417 --> 00:34:08.989
And to some degree, you know,

00:34:08.989 --> 00:34:10.690
we have a lot of good things as humans,

00:34:10.690 --> 00:34:12.660
like, you know, air conditioners

00:34:12.660 --> 00:34:14.320
and you know, sometimes computers

00:34:14.320 --> 00:34:15.153
and things like that.

00:34:15.153 --> 00:34:16.709
But like, we oftentimes
create our own stresses

00:34:16.709 --> 00:34:19.031
in our brain, and you know,

00:34:19.031 --> 00:34:21.260
the real takeaway is,

00:34:21.260 --> 00:34:22.093
if you take that away

00:34:22.093 --> 00:34:23.630
and you just be your authentic self

00:34:23.630 --> 00:34:24.700
and you just always put one foot

00:34:24.700 --> 00:34:25.533
in front of the other

00:34:25.533 --> 00:34:27.369
and just try to make do of what you have,

00:34:27.369 --> 00:34:29.210
I've quoted frozen to in the past,

00:34:29.210 --> 00:34:30.420
to do the next right thing,

00:34:30.420 --> 00:34:32.930
then not only will you
probably be enjoying life more,

00:34:32.930 --> 00:34:34.180
but probably other people

00:34:34.180 --> 00:34:37.092
will want you to be
part of their community

00:34:37.092 --> 00:34:38.940
or gravitate to you.

00:34:38.940 --> 00:34:40.470
So it's a win-win.

00:34:40.470 --> 00:34:41.967
So with that, thanks for joining

00:34:41.967 --> 00:34:44.250
and I believe tomorrow we're gonna have

